# Jupyter, DSLs, and Separating Causes that have Different Effects

One of the broader goals of the [Helical project](/projects.html#helical) is to make writing, maintaining, and debugging experiments easier and safer for the end-user. However, learning a new programming language can itself be a barrier. To address this concern, last year a Northeastern coop student ([Kevin G. Yang]()) investigated the suitability of using Jupyter notebooks as an execution environment for experiments. 

<!--more-->

Jupyter notebooks are commonly used by empiricists. I hypothesized that providing IDE-like support in Jupyter notebooks would reduce the effects of language learning/the novelty of Helical on end-users' ability to write experiments.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    LN(Language Novelty) --> EE(Ease of Expression)
    EC(Experiment Complexity) --> EE
```

